from qlib import Q
C='property-characteristics'
Q(C,'basic',"Which of the following is usually considered real property?","A built-in dishwasher installed in the kitchen",[("A sofa in the living room","Furniture that is not attached is personal property."),("A riding lawn mower kept in the garage","Movable equipment is personal property."),("A framed painting hanging on a nail","Items hung on a nail can be removed easily and are generally personal property.")],"Attached fixture = real property","A **fixture** is an item that was personal property but has been attached to the land or building so that it becomes part of the real property. A dishwasher built into the cabinets and connected to water and power is a typical fixture and normally goes with the house when it is sold.",4,["fixtures"])
Q(C,'basic',"Crops that a tenant farmer plants and harvests each year are called:","Emblements",[("Fixtures","Fixtures are items attached to the land or a building, not annual crops."),("Easements","An easement is a right to use another person's land."),("Appurtenances","Appurtenances are rights that go with the land, such as an easement, not the farmer's annual crops.")],"Emblements = annual crops planted by labor","**Emblements** are crops that need yearly planting and work, such as corn or wheat. They are treated as personal property of the person who planted them, so a tenant farmer may usually come back to harvest them even after the lease ends.",4,["emblements"])
Q(C,'intermediate',"A restaurant tenant installed ovens and booths for use in its business. When the lease ends, these items are generally:","Trade fixtures that the tenant may remove before the lease ends",[("Real property that now belongs to the landlord","Trade fixtures are an exception; they remain the tenant's personal property."),("Emblements that belong to the tenant only until harvest","Emblements are crops, not business equipment."),("Encroachments that must be left in place","An encroachment is an improvement that crosses onto a neighbor's land.")],"Trade fixtures = tenant's business items, removable","**Trade fixtures** are items a tenant attaches for use in a trade or business. They stay the tenant's personal property and can be removed before the lease ends, as long as the tenant repairs any damage. Items left behind after the lease ends may become the landlord's property.",4,["trade fixtures"])
Q(C,'intermediate',"Courts often use the acronym MARIA to decide whether an item is a fixture. What does the \"A\" for \"adaptation\" ask?","Whether the item was specially fitted for use in that building",[("Whether the item was paid for in cash","How an item was paid for is not part of the fixture test."),("Whether the item is older than the building","Age is not one of the fixture tests."),("Whether the buyer asked about the item at the showing","The buyer's questions do not decide whether something is a fixture.")],"MARIA: Method, Adaptation, Relationship, Intention, Agreement","The MARIA tests are **M**ethod of attachment, **A**daptation to the property, **R**elationship of the parties, **I**ntention of the person who attached it, and **A**greement between the parties. Adaptation asks whether the item was custom-made or specially fitted for that property, such as storm windows cut to size.",3,["fixtures"])
Q(C,'advanced',"A seller wants to keep a chandelier that is wired into the dining room ceiling. What is the best way to handle this?","State in the purchase contract that the chandelier is excluded from the sale",[("Say nothing, because light fixtures always stay with the seller","Wired-in light fixtures are generally real property and would transfer with the house."),("Tell the buyer verbally at the showing","Agreements about real property should be in writing in the contract to be enforceable and clear."),("Remove it after closing","After closing, the chandelier belongs to the buyer unless the contract excluded it.")],"Exclude fixtures in writing in the contract","A chandelier wired into the ceiling is normally a **fixture** and goes with the house. Because the written **agreement** of the parties controls, the seller should exclude it in the purchase contract, or remove and replace it before the house is shown.",4,["fixtures","contracts"])
Q(C,'basic',"Which physical characteristic of land means that no two parcels are exactly alike?","Non-homogeneity (uniqueness)",[("Immobility","Immobility means land cannot be moved."),("Indestructibility","Indestructibility means land itself cannot be destroyed."),("Scarcity","Scarcity is an economic characteristic about limited supply.")],"Uniqueness = non-homogeneity","The three **physical** characteristics of land are immobility, indestructibility, and **non-homogeneity (uniqueness)**. Because every parcel has a different location, no two are exactly the same. This is why courts can order specific performance of a real estate contract.",4,["characteristics of land"])
Q(C,'intermediate',"People prefer a certain neighborhood, so similar homes there sell for more than homes in another area. This economic characteristic is called:","Area preference (situs)",[("Permanence of investment","Permanence of investment refers to long-lasting improvements such as roads and sewers."),("Immobility","Immobility is a physical characteristic, not a buyer preference."),("Indestructibility","Indestructibility means land cannot be destroyed.")],"Situs = area preference, the most important factor in value","**Area preference**, also called **situs**, means people's choices about location. It is an **economic** characteristic of land and is the reason location has such a strong effect on value.",3,["characteristics of land"])
Q(C,'basic',"Which method of legal description uses compass directions and distances from a point of beginning?","Metes and bounds",[("Lot and block","Lot and block refers to a lot number on a recorded plat map."),("Rectangular (government) survey","The rectangular survey uses townships, ranges, and sections."),("Street address","A street address is not a legal description.")],"Metes and bounds = directions + distances, returns to start","A **metes and bounds** description starts at a **point of beginning**, follows directions (bounds) and distances (metes) around the parcel, and must return to the point of beginning to close.",5,["legal descriptions"])
Q(C,'intermediate',"In the rectangular (government) survey system, how many sections are in a standard township?","36",[("24","A township is 6 miles by 6 miles, which makes 36 one-mile sections."),("640","640 is the number of acres in one section, not the number of sections."),("16","16 is too few; a township has 6 rows of 6 sections.")],"Township = 6 × 6 miles = 36 sections","A standard **township** is 6 miles on each side. It is divided into one-mile-square **sections**, so it has 6 × 6 = **36** sections. Each section contains 640 acres.",5,["legal descriptions"])
Q(C,'intermediate',"A section in the rectangular survey system contains how many acres?","640 acres",[("43,560 acres","43,560 is the number of square feet in one acre."),("36 acres","36 is the number of sections in a township."),("160 acres","160 acres is a quarter section.")],"1 section = 1 square mile = 640 acres","One **section** is one square mile and contains **640 acres**. A quarter section is 160 acres. Remember that one acre is 43,560 square feet.",5,["legal descriptions"])
Q(C,'advanced',"How many acres are in the NE 1/4 of the SW 1/4 of a section?","40 acres",[("160 acres","160 acres is a full quarter section; this description takes a quarter of a quarter."),("80 acres","80 acres would be a half of a quarter section."),("10 acres","10 acres would be a quarter of a quarter of a quarter section.")],"Multiply fractions × 640 acres","Work from the end of the description back to the section:\n\n1. One section = 640 acres.\n2. SW 1/4 = 640 ÷ 4 = 160 acres.\n3. NE 1/4 of that = 160 ÷ 4 = **40 acres**.\n4. Check: 640 × 1/4 × 1/4 = 40.",5,["legal descriptions","math"])
Q(C,'advanced',"Which of the following is NOT a valid legal description of land?","The street address and tax bill mailing address",[("A metes and bounds description","Metes and bounds is a recognized legal description."),("A lot and block number on a recorded plat","Lot and block is a recognized legal description."),("A rectangular survey description","The government survey system is a recognized legal description.")],"Street address is not a legal description","The three accepted methods are **metes and bounds**, **lot and block (recorded plat)**, and the **rectangular (government) survey**. A street address or mailing address can change and does not exactly locate the boundaries, so it is not a legal description.",4,["legal descriptions"],lock=True)
C='land-use-controls'
Q(C,'basic',"The government's power to take private property for public use, with just compensation, is called:","Eminent domain",[("Escheat","Escheat is when property goes to the state because the owner died without a will or heirs."),("Police power","Police power is the right to regulate property, such as zoning, without taking ownership."),("Taxation","Taxation is the power to charge taxes on property.")],"Eminent domain = taking for public use + just compensation","**Eminent domain** lets the government take private property for public use, such as a highway, but it must pay the owner **just compensation**. The legal process of taking the property is called **condemnation**.",5,["government powers"])
Q(C,'basic',"When a person dies without a will and without heirs, the property passes to the state by:","Escheat",[("Eminent domain","Eminent domain is a taking for public use with compensation."),("Adverse possession","Adverse possession is gaining title by open, continuous use of another's land."),("Condemnation","Condemnation is the legal process used for eminent domain.")],"Escheat = no will, no heirs → state","**Escheat** returns property to the state when an owner dies **without a will and without heirs**, or when property is abandoned under state law.",5,["government powers"])
Q(C,'intermediate',"Zoning ordinances are an example of which government power?","Police power",[("Eminent domain","Zoning regulates use; it does not take ownership."),("Escheat","Escheat concerns property of owners with no heirs."),("Taxation","Zoning is not a tax.")],"Zoning = police power","**Police power** is the government's right to make rules that protect public health, safety, and welfare. Zoning, building codes, and subdivision rules are all uses of police power. No payment to the owner is required.",5,["zoning"])
Q(C,'intermediate',"A store that existed before a new zoning ordinance was passed and is allowed to continue even though it does not meet the new rules is called a:","Nonconforming use",[("Variance","A variance is permission to differ from the zoning rules, usually because of a hardship."),("Conditional use permit","A conditional (special) use permit allows a use that the zone permits only under certain conditions."),("Spot zoning","Spot zoning singles out one parcel for different zoning and is often illegal.")],"Grandfathered use = nonconforming use","A **nonconforming use** is a use that was legal before the zoning changed. It is usually allowed to continue (\"grandfathered\"), but it may not be expanded and may be lost if abandoned or destroyed.",4,["zoning"])
Q(C,'intermediate',"An owner's lot is oddly shaped, and meeting the required setback would make building almost impossible. The owner should ask the zoning board for a:","Variance",[("Nonconforming use","A nonconforming use is a use that existed before the zoning changed."),("Deed restriction","A deed restriction is a private limit, not permission from the zoning board."),("Down zoning","Down zoning is a change to a less intense use for an area.")],"Hardship from strict zoning → variance","A **variance** allows an owner to depart from a zoning requirement, such as a setback, when strict rules would cause an unusual hardship because of the property's shape or conditions.",4,["zoning"])
Q(C,'basic',"A private rule in a subdivision that limits homes to one story is an example of a:","Deed restriction (CC&R)",[("Zoning ordinance","Zoning is a public rule made by the local government."),("Building code","Building codes set construction standards and are public rules."),("Easement","An easement is a right to use land, not a limit on the height of homes.")],"CC&Rs = private land use controls","**Covenants, conditions, and restrictions (CC&Rs)** are **private** controls placed by a developer or owner, usually recorded with the subdivision plat or in deeds. Homeowners' associations often enforce them.",4,["private controls"])
Q(C,'intermediate',"A utility company has the right to run power lines across many properties. This right is an example of an:","Easement in gross",[("Easement appurtenant","An easement appurtenant benefits a neighboring parcel of land, not a company."),("Encroachment","An encroachment is an improvement that crosses onto another's land without permission."),("License","A license is temporary personal permission that can be revoked.")],"Utility easement = easement in gross","An **easement in gross** benefits a person or company rather than a neighboring parcel. Utility easements are the most common example. An **easement appurtenant** has a dominant parcel (which benefits) and a servient parcel (which carries the burden).",4,["easements"])
Q(C,'advanced',"A neighbor's new fence was built two feet onto a homeowner's lot. A survey shows this. The fence is best described as an:","Encroachment",[("Easement by necessity","An easement by necessity gives access to a landlocked parcel."),("License","A license is permission; the neighbor had no permission."),("Appurtenance","An appurtenance is a right that goes with the land.")],"Improvement over the line = encroachment","An **encroachment** is a building, fence, or other improvement that extends onto another person's land without permission. A survey usually reveals it. If ignored for a long time, it may lead to a claim of adverse possession or a prescriptive easement under state law.",4,["encroachments"])
Q(C,'advanced',"Which of the following is a temporary, revocable permission to use land that does not create an interest in the land?","A license",[("An easement appurtenant","An easement appurtenant is an interest in land that runs with the land."),("A life estate","A life estate is an ownership interest."),("An easement by prescription","A prescriptive easement is gained by long, open use and is not freely revocable.")],"License = personal, revocable permission","A **license** is personal permission to do something on another's land, such as a ticket to a concert or permission to fish in a pond. It can usually be revoked and ends when the owner dies or sells the land.",3,["licenses"])
